frames: 70 taken in the run (15 s, ISO 3200 by the RAW's own EXIF; every one plate-solved on M27 by its sidecar); 66 used, 4 dropped
dropped: {'20261018-034619': 'the first frame after the slew to the target (52 s after it): dropped by rule; it measured stars of 4.64 arcsec, smear 0.07, transparency 1.000', '20261018-035059': 'smeared: every star stretched the same way, common ellipticity 0.21 at 22 deg (limit 0.20); median elongation 1.35', '20261018-035232': 'smeared: every star stretched the same way, common ellipticity 0.20 at 10 deg (limit 0.20); median elongation 1.32', '20261018-041416': 'smeared: every star stretched the same way, common ellipticity 0.21 at -31 deg (limit 0.20); median elongation 1.32'}
not_in_the_run: before 03:46 UTC: centring frames (2 s at ISO 6400, a 1/60 s frame) and the focusing frames on Vega (2 s, 03:06 to 03:25); from 04:23 UTC, M33 (another target, used only for a candidate flat). None of these is in the stack
integration_s: 990.0
integration: 66 x 15 s = 16.5 minutes
transparency: 0.980 to 1.009 of the run's typical star flux: no cloud
star_size: half-flux diameter, green, 117 compact unsaturated stars: 4.47 arcsec in the stack, 4.94 in the reference frame alone; the run's frames 4.64 (median, step 4). The box's own figure (the 960 px JPEG preview) read 6.2 to 7.3 arcsec for the same frames: a coarser measure of the same stars
flat: the 13 October hybrid flat (~/.observatory/nights/2026-10-12-a6000/flat-hybrid-m77: large scale from that night's open sky of NGC 891, NGC 2261, NGC 2419 and M1; dust from the 13 October twilight flats), chosen over two others on this run's own sky (step 7a), times this run's own measure of the dust that moved since (step 7)
dust: three shadows named for tonight, their depth in this run's own sky before any flat / after the 13 October flat / after this run's own correction: crescent at sensor (3534, 824): 12.7% / 12.6% / 1.7%; spot_a at sensor (1356, 2400): 5.5% / 0.5% / 0.1%; spot_b at sensor (5352, 3456): 9.0% / 0.2% / 0.2%. The crescent is new since 13 October (the twilight flat has only a 3% shadow there) and is taken out by this run's own measure; the two spots were already in the 13 October flat. 11 shadows in all were measured on this run's sky (seen in the odd and in the even frames alone) and divided out, 0.73% of the sensor
darks: none taken tonight: darks.subtract changed nothing and said so; hot pixels found from the run itself and repaired by the 3 x 3 median of their colour plane
combine: proper coaddition (Zackay & Ofek 2017; tools/coadd.py, keep_phase="mean"), 269 PSF stars per plane, one PSF per frame and plane; theory S/N gain over the old weighted mean 1.0100 (green). The old combine is made from the same cube as a control
noise: sky noise per stack pixel 6.2 to 8.2 DN against 48.1 to 60.8 for one frame: 7.45 to 7.75 times lower (8.12 expected from the weights)
is_it_the_thing: yes: the reference frame and the stack plate-solved (astrometry.net); M27's central star's catalogue place falls 0.7 arcsec from the light's centroid in the stack
ears: the faint outer lobes ("ears", filling the nebula out along its long axis, position angle about 90 to 150 and 270 to 330 degrees) are real: 3 to 4 arcmin from the star they are 12/12, 11/11, 6/6, 4/4, 12/12, 12/12, 5/5 sigma above blank sky in the odd and in the even frames alone (step 12). Beyond 4 arcmin no sector passes in either half: the faint outer halo seen in deep pictures (out to about 7 arcmin) is NOT seen in 16.5 minutes from this sky
followed: the nebula followed (ring median of green above 3 x the blank-sky spread, unbroken from the star) to 4.04 arcmin from the star, 8.1 arcmin across
colour: the camera's daylight white balance times Gaia DR3 star colours (kR 1.0128, kB 1.1530 from 93 stars), the camera's own colour matrix. The nebula's blue-green (doubly ionised oxygen, 501 nm) is outside sRGB; colour shown only above 8 DN, grey below. No saturation beyond x 1.2
picture: a 1200 x 1000 px cut at the stack's own pixels (0.777 arcsec per px, 15.5 x 12.9 arcmin), nothing resampled after the stack, never enlarged; north 116.2 degrees clockwise from up (the camera's orientation)
sky: dark sky neutral: m27.png                      darkest  0-10%: R   1.0 G   1.0 B   1.0 (R-G  +0.0, B-G  +0.0) | darkest 10-40%: R   2.8 G   2.8 B   2.8 (R-G  +0.0, B-G  -0.0)
jpeg_metadata_free: True
altitude: about 60 to 70 degrees up during the run (sidecars: 60 to 66); no clock time goes with it anywhere published
